[[:db/retract 17592186304668 :answer-field/choices 17592186302791] [:db/retract 17592186304668 :answer-field/choices 17592186302840] [:db/retract 17592186304668 :answer-field/choices 17592186303130] [:db/retract 17592186304668 :answer-field/choices 17592186302652] [:db/retract 17592186304668 :answer-field/choices 17592186303710] {:db/id 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4e106ec5-d244-5095-9a3e-86feed9bed83"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4e106ec5-d244-5095-9a3e-86feed9bed83" :question/id #uuid "4e106ec5-d244-5095-9a3e-86feed9bed83" :question/math-academy-id "q-332687" :question/problem "![](images/39/390321da0e4c3367d121a1561ccc41ca4960c912ff53d909acd1c90f505758ce.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-540f62cb-7d1e-5455-b752-8d1fe82b863d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-540f62cb-7d1e-5455-b752-8d1fe82b863d" :answer-field/id #uuid "540f62cb-7d1e-5455-b752-8d1fe82b863d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed7d8b7f-1e93-5e81-b22e-1d876ddc07f9" "answer-85b1285c-0fbc-5e41-b4e0-700ef6b3681e" "answer-62f8b741-a9bc-5f56-8242-c18d258a8e1e" "answer-8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" "answer-40549b82-d47f-561c-8931-d7d35ef461cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed7d8b7f-1e93-5e81-b22e-1d876ddc07f9" :answer/id #uuid "ed7d8b7f-1e93-5e81-b22e-1d876ddc07f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85b1285c-0fbc-5e41-b4e0-700ef6b3681e" :answer/id #uuid "85b1285c-0fbc-5e41-b4e0-700ef6b3681e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62f8b741-a9bc-5f56-8242-c18d258a8e1e" :answer/id #uuid "62f8b741-a9bc-5f56-8242-c18d258a8e1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" :answer/id #uuid "8fc563a4-4937-522e-9fb1-2f13c18af602" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40549b82-d47f-561c-8931-d7d35ef461cb" :answer/id #uuid "40549b82-d47f-561c-8931-d7d35ef461cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186305096 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304668 :answer-field/correct "answer-6228cec8-4f90-5557-9758-248e08a02159" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2af9e8ee-427e-5b4a-9c20-64d8c764d689" "answer-ad7adf14-40d3-5a8b-b017-a61006c8a570" "answer-e2fef36d-c8ab-5030-a6ef-407316ba8a3e" "answer-6228cec8-4f90-5557-9758-248e08a02159" "answer-4cfdedb2-5206-5bb7-bc0c-b4380ef84ac3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2af9e8ee-427e-5b4a-9c20-64d8c764d689" :answer/id #uuid "2af9e8ee-427e-5b4a-9c20-64d8c764d689" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad7adf14-40d3-5a8b-b017-a61006c8a570" :answer/id #uuid "ad7adf14-40d3-5a8b-b017-a61006c8a570" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2fef36d-c8ab-5030-a6ef-407316ba8a3e" :answer/id #uuid "e2fef36d-c8ab-5030-a6ef-407316ba8a3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6228cec8-4f90-5557-9758-248e08a02159" :answer/id #uuid "6228cec8-4f90-5557-9758-248e08a02159" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cfdedb2-5206-5bb7-bc0c-b4380ef84ac3" :answer/id #uuid "4cfdedb2-5206-5bb7-bc0c-b4380ef84ac3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-97c6c7fa-18db-5e5d-84d1-3076bd6f5c92"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186391144 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-97c6c7fa-18db-5e5d-84d1-3076bd6f5c92" :question/id #uuid "97c6c7fa-18db-5e5d-84d1-3076bd6f5c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-333183" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2c81ff3c50013c149dc6a5db67bbe11cccdbbef22ce2c0712101f4a358bcb8c.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{2t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{2t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{t})^{′}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}}{{c}_{1}{e}^{t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cfc6238d-540c-5d03-81af-c08e27ebdfce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cfc6238d-540c-5d03-81af-c08e27ebdfce" :answer-field/id #uuid "cfc6238d-540c-5d03-81af-c08e27ebdfce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd125c4d-09c0-5767-a865-91e186cba8d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62879d01-d1d9-55e2-ab3d-537c10ca44a9" "answer-24461d44-24c5-575d-be64-3d0e46a32765" "answer-ce200fd8-672b-5314-9ac6-4d8b99ea280c" "answer-dd125c4d-09c0-5767-a865-91e186cba8d5" "answer-c46723d7-b8d3-5ade-85f9-0bf37a1a2dc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62879d01-d1d9-55e2-ab3d-537c10ca44a9" :answer/id #uuid "62879d01-d1d9-55e2-ab3d-537c10ca44a9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/19/196d1a61793b619bf86f3ed18852f819cd27821e0ad0388bcd05621e60ba46ba.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24461d44-24c5-575d-be64-3d0e46a32765" :answer/id #uuid "24461d44-24c5-575d-be64-3d0e46a32765" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/7a/7aaaf92ef853dcc88c787120fbb380a1529a49504255fb1b05803e3712587c90.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce200fd8-672b-5314-9ac6-4d8b99ea280c" :answer/id #uuid "ce200fd8-672b-5314-9ac6-4d8b99ea280c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/8c/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd125c4d-09c0-5767-a865-91e186cba8d5" :answer/id #uuid "dd125c4d-09c0-5767-a865-91e186cba8d5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/9a/9ab329eed7e67e017a822e8d5163b331e0c93a9747f74aee3a10d6592f44a59b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c46723d7-b8d3-5ade-85f9-0bf37a1a2dc2" :answer/id #uuid "c46723d7-b8d3-5ade-85f9-0bf37a1a2dc2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f1/f1162ad9109f4117ea94321ee1e2192734b3a895b35232a0788c0ff754e2bade.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2cc95b14-28b1-5770-bd6e-4ea958da90bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2cc95b14-28b1-5770-bd6e-4ea958da90bb" :question/id #uuid "2cc95b14-28b1-5770-bd6e-4ea958da90bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-333222" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/665ff96d191a9b2dc6dc57f1c3a97743dcb1a26c4118151716bdf430a4c014f3.png)" :q